islature and you  ask for less regulation and you have K  Sport you can often get that done unless  there's some other lobby against you or  there's some pressing need we've heard a  lot of talk from Democrats and from  President Obama themselves saying that  their major goal is to turn Texas blue  do you see that happening and no not  this year for sure maybe in 2018 they  always talk about in terms of  demographics the states getting younger  it's a going to be I think majority  Hispanic in the next few years it's  already majority minority quote-unquote  it's getting more urbanized it's getting  more educated so you're starting to see  a different kind of constituency in the  big cities but so far no we're not  seeing Democrats put out the candidates  or the campaigns or really the message  the Republicans aren't either which is  kinda interesting there's a couple good  ones coming out but given how well the  state has done I've been surprised by  how the state role can party in this law  set of primaries is focused on issues  that nobody cares about like the 17th  amendment  or you know further marginal abortion  restrictions or further gun laws and I  think those are you know interesting  policy debates but really not the key  issues in a state growing this much and  that's this big I will get Texas and you  know this is a state that gave us Ron  Paul gave us Ross Perot do you see any  more libertarian independent type  politician here that could break out on  the national level well this is an  interesting question so I think that a  lot of Texas Republicans a lot of Texans  in general are sort of quasi libertarian  or tacitly libertarian because of the  issues they prioritize right so even  someone like Perry he's a conservative  he's a fiscal conservative social  conservative but if you think about the  course of his administration and it's  been more than decade um he's really put  fiscal issues first he hasn't really  advanced social issues and I think tha